WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.690
Since the former Soviet Union launched the Sputnik 1 satellite in 1957.

00:00:04.690 --> 00:00:06.177
A new moon is in the sky.

00:00:06.177 --> 00:00:09.202
More than 10,000 objects have been sent into space.

00:00:09.202 --> 00:00:16.435
10,000 is actually a relatively small number, compared with what’s to come.

00:00:16.435 --> 00:00:18.011
Go USA!

00:00:19.809 --> 00:00:26.413
Beginninig in 2019, SpaceX has been using the Falcon 9 to put satellites into orbit for a global internet.

00:00:26.413 --> 00:00:30.399
There we go, as they make their way off.

00:00:30.399 --> 00:00:32.890
It has now launched nearly a thousand.

00:00:32.890 --> 00:00:43.713
The plan is to put at least 12,000 Starlink satellites into orbit and the US Federal Communications Commission, the FCC, has given the green light for up to 42,000.

00:00:44.406 --> 00:00:50.476
They will soon have some company as Amazon has plans to offer a competing service, dubbed Project Kuiper.

00:00:50.476 --> 00:00:55.971
Leading Elon Musk to call the online retailer’s founder Jeff Bezos a copycat.

00:00:55.971 --> 00:01:03.773
The billionaire entrepreneurs have the same game plan. They want to beam high-speed satellite internet to remote and rural areas on Earth.

00:01:03.773 --> 00:01:09.806
Amazon has a lot of catching up to do. It hasn't launched any satellites, but it does have the means to get them there.

00:01:11.297 --> 00:01:15.807
It just so happens that Bezos has another company, Blue Origin.

00:01:15.807 --> 00:01:24.453
His space venture has yet to go into orbit but it has signed deals to launch satellites for Canada’s Telesat and the UK’s OneWeb using New Glenn.

00:01:24.919 --> 00:01:28.783
The orbital rocket is set to fly for the first time in 2021.

00:01:28.783 --> 00:01:37.043
Amazon plans to launch 3,200 satellites - roughly a quarter of SpaceX’s current goal. The FCC gave the go-ahead on the condition

00:01:37.043 --> 00:01:41.300
that they don’t interfere with previously authorized projects, like Starlink.

00:01:41.806 --> 00:01:50.485
But Amazon is worried that SpaceX might actually interfere with their satellites after Musk’s company wanted to move a batch of Starlinks to a lower orbit.

00:01:50.907 --> 00:01:57.229
SpaceX will now operate all of its satellites at least a kilometer below Amazon’s to make sure there’s no interference.

00:01:57.229 --> 00:02:04.648
Both companies are flying only 500 - 600 km away from our planet whereas current satellite networks are too far away,

00:02:04.648 --> 00:02:12.618
sometimes tens of thousands of kilometers out. The closer they are to Earth, the lower the latency, which measures responsiveness and lag. 

00:02:12.618 --> 00:02:17.264
The time it takes between clicking on a keyboard and getting a response on screen.

00:02:17.264 --> 00:02:22.986
A downside of being closer is you see less of the planet, so more satellites are needed.

00:02:22.986 --> 00:02:29.140
SpaceX has already launched enough to beta test its service in the northern United States and parts of Canada.

00:02:29.140 --> 00:02:34.397
Among the first private customers is a native American tribe in a remote part of Washington state.

00:02:34.397 --> 00:02:38.346
They received a terminal the size of a pizza to connect to Starlink.

00:02:38.346 --> 00:02:45.821
SpaceX says latency ranges from 20 to 40 milliseconds but expects that to fall below 20 as more satellites are launched.

00:02:45.821 --> 00:02:48.445
That’s enough to have a good gaming experience.

00:02:48.445 --> 00:02:55.204
It says data speeds ranging from 50 Mb/s to 150 Mb/s are expected to increase dramatically as well.

00:02:55.204 --> 00:02:59.495
The current speeds are more than enough to stream a YouTube video in 4K.

00:02:59.495 --> 00:03:03.204
Amazon also says it's been able to stream 4K video. 

00:03:03.204 --> 00:03:10.728
The company announced this antenna it developed connected to a satellite 50 times farther out than where its own satellites will be deployed.

00:03:10.728 --> 00:03:17.966
Tests at its research facility in Redmond, Washington found the antenna can handle up to 400 Mbps

00:03:17.966 --> 00:03:21.096
though it hasn’t said how good the internet connection will be.

00:03:21.096 --> 00:03:28.311
The prototype is just a foot in diameter because the company explains it will be cheaper to manufacture and less expensive for the customer.

00:03:28.311 --> 00:03:33.292
We don’t know how much Amazon’s service will cost, and whether it will be similar in price to Starlink. 

00:03:33.292 --> 00:03:35.953
Some people believe Starlink is too expensive.

00:03:35.953 --> 00:03:41.633
The initial service is priced at $99 a month plus $499 (emphasize!) for the equipment.

00:03:41.633 --> 00:03:46.912
Musk has said the most difficult technical challenge is bringing the price of the satellite dish down.

00:03:46.912 --> 00:03:55.355
SpaceX does hope to attract customers globally. The plan is to expand coverage to nearly all parts of the populated planet in 2021.

00:03:55.355 --> 00:04:02.354
Amazon also has ambitions to connect the world, starting with the US. When that will happen is still unclear.

00:04:02.354 --> 00:04:11.324
In order to meet FCC requirements, Amazon has to get half of its satellites into orbit and operational by 2026, with the rest up and running three years later.

00:04:11.324 --> 00:04:18.381
Besides beaming internet directly to customers, Amazon and SpaceX hope to provide backhaul solutions to telecom companies

00:04:18.381 --> 00:04:21.872
so they can extend their 5G coverage to hard to reach places.

00:04:23.136 --> 00:04:27.689
Building a satellite internet service takes a lot of effort and even more money.

00:04:27.885 --> 00:04:32.323
Amazon says it’s investing more than $10 billion into Project Kuiper -

00:04:32.323 --> 00:04:38.283
precisely the amount SpaceX’s COO Gwynne Shotwell once estimated would be needed to complete Starlink.

00:04:38.283 --> 00:04:43.287
The upfront costs may be high, but the returns are expected to be far greater.

00:04:44.328 --> 00:04:52.081
SpaceX could become a $100 billion dollar company thanks largely to Starlink, according to the investment banking giant Morgan Stanley.

00:04:52.081 --> 00:04:59.325
Musk is counting on its success to help fund his quest to send people to Mars on this giant rocket, Starship.

00:04:59.325 --> 00:05:07.821
As for Amazon, Kuiper could further expand Amazon’s worldwide customer base. The service could be bundled as part of a package under the Amazon umbrella.

00:05:07.821 --> 00:05:15.099
Amazon Web Services already offers computing and storage to clients like Netflix. So the new internet could help move the data.

00:05:15.099 --> 00:05:22.839
Despite the rivalry between Musk and Bezos, satellite internet could rake in billions of dollars for both of their companies.

00:05:22.839 --> 00:05:25.117
And provide more choice for consumers.

00:05:25.117 --> 00:05:31.938
No matter which internet provider you choose, you might run into a situation where you can’t access certain sites or videos.

